From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:59:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/apple: m4: Bound firmware calls by progress, not
 by one fixed total

The IOMFB startup is one outer call (A401) inside which the firmware
issues a few hundred nested callbacks (317 on the 26.6.2 firmware) before
it replies. dcp_m4_runtime_call_stream() armed a single deadline of 5 s
for the outermost call and every nested call inherited it, so the whole
startup had to finish within 5 s no matter how steadily the firmware was
progressing.

That budget is thin. Measured on a T8132 MacBook Pro (J604) with the
same firmware, from the INIT message to "IOMFB startup complete":
3.48 s and 4.20 s on two runtime binds long after boot. When the DCP is
adopted at boot (1.3 s after kernel start), every firmware step arrived
1.13-1.26x later than in the runtime binds (other drivers probing in
parallel), and the startup was cut off with -ETIMEDOUT exactly 5.00 s
after INIT. The firmware had not stalled: it was inside the last D121
(start_hardware_boot) leg, which took 1.46 s in the runtime bind. The
display then fell back to simpledrm.

Bound each wait by progress instead. A waiting call still gives the
firmware 5 s (DCP_M4_STEP_MS, unchanged) for its next message, but every
message received (a nested callback or the reply) re-arms that wait.
A hard cap of 15 s (DCP_M4_CALL_LIMIT_MS) after the outermost call began
still ends a firmware that keeps calling back without ever replying.

The cap: at the measured boot-time rate, startup should take about
5.0-5.3 s (4.20 s x 1.26). 15 s is about 3x that and 3.6x the slowest
measured success, so ordinary jitter does not hit it. It still stops a
broken startup within a time userspace can wait out: display managers
commonly wait about 10 s for a DRM device before falling back.
The longest measured gap between consecutive firmware log lines during
startup was 1.79 s, and each such gap contains several callbacks, so the
unchanged 5 s per-message wait keeps a wide margin and still catches a
silent firmware in 5 s.

Only calls that start with no deadline set (the startup calls and the
outermost calls after it) get the cap. Swaps and the idle drain still
set their own fixed deadline first, and a nested call never shortens or
resets the outer call's deadline, so their behaviour is unchanged.

Also log the total startup time, so slow startups are visible before they
fail.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
(cherry picked from commit 945c5511ed55ff6f373b54e949ad732ea4576c3b)
[j604: k610 drm sync: iomfb_m4.c conflicts with the upstream external-DCP
 code: the defines go after the upstream dcp_m4_ext_* types, and
 dcp_m4_runtime_call_stream() keeps the upstream ext_local_calls /
 ext_call_epoch bookkeeping around the progress-bounded deadline. The
 upstream fixed-total deadlines (swaps, ext top-level calls) leave
 r->limit at 0, so they are not re-armed and keep their behaviour.]
(cherry picked from commit 9b32bb94f32c57f805f5863a75a6dd95a8c811a8)
(cherry picked from commit 8210c5b5ef4093d76c1e8c074154cc5c495b6e55)
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c | 46 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c
index d0f9f6fb88..59c8c179ec 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c
@@ -807,6 +807,17 @@ struct dcp_m4_ext_done {
 	u32 hol[4];
 };
 
+/*
+ * A firmware call is bounded by its progress, not by one fixed total. While
+ * the host waits, the firmware has DCP_M4_STEP_MS for its next message; each
+ * message received (the reply or one more nested callback) re-arms that wait,
+ * but never past DCP_M4_CALL_LIMIT_MS after the outermost call began. A
+ * silent firmware still fails after DCP_M4_STEP_MS, and one that keeps
+ * calling back without ever replying fails at the hard cap.
+ */
+#define DCP_M4_STEP_MS		5000
+#define DCP_M4_CALL_LIMIT_MS	15000
+
 struct dcp_m4_runtime {
 	struct iomfb_m4 *dcp;
 	struct dcp_m4_properties *properties;
@@ -850,6 +861,8 @@ struct dcp_m4_runtime {
 	int ext_restore_sink_gen;
 	u32 head, count, callbacks;
 	unsigned long deadline;
+	/* Hard cap of a progress-bounded call; 0: deadline is a fixed total. */
+	unsigned long limit;
 	bool done;
 	bool background_started, background_done;
 	u32 background_id;
@@ -991,6 +1004,14 @@ static void dcp_m4_runtime_receive(struct iomfb_m4 *dcp, u64 word)
 		queue_work(r->wq, &r->idle);
 }
 
+/* A firmware message is progress: re-arm the step wait, never past the cap. */
+static void dcp_m4_runtime_progress(struct dcp_m4_runtime *r)
+{
+	unsigned long next = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(DCP_M4_STEP_MS);
+
+	r->deadline = time_before(next, r->limit) ? next : r->limit;
+}
+
 /* Called only while holding dcp->lock, by the synchronous RPC owner or idle worker. */
 static int dcp_m4_runtime_next(struct dcp_m4_runtime *r, u64 *word, bool wait)
 {
@@ -1017,6 +1038,8 @@ static int dcp_m4_runtime_next(struct dcp_m4_runtime *r, u64 *word, bool wait)
 		r->count--;
 	}
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dcp->state_lock, flags);
+	if (!ret && r->limit)
+		dcp_m4_runtime_progress(r);
 	return ret;
 }
 
@@ -1893,8 +1916,10 @@ static int dcp_m4_runtime_call_stream(struct dcp_m4_runtime *r, unsigned int met
 		r->ext_local_calls++;
 		r->ext_call_epoch = atomic_read(&dcp->ext_pm_epoch);
 	}
-	if (!r->deadline)
-		r->deadline = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(5000);
+	if (!r->deadline) {
+		r->deadline = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(DCP_M4_STEP_MS);
+		r->limit = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(DCP_M4_CALL_LIMIT_MS);
+	}
 	if (time_after_eq(jiffies, r->deadline)) {
 		ret = -ETIMEDOUT;
 		goto finished;
@@ -1924,11 +1949,18 @@ static int dcp_m4_runtime_call_stream(struct dcp_m4_runtime *r, unsigned int met
 		ret = dcp_m4_runtime_callback(r, word);
 	}
 finished:
-	r->deadline = previous;
 	if (!remote) {
 		r->ext_local_calls--;
 		r->ext_call_epoch = previous_epoch;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * A nested call leaves the deadline its messages re-armed to the outer
+	 * call (a fixed deadline is unchanged by it); the outermost call ends both.
+	 */
+	if (!previous) {
+		r->deadline = 0;
+		r->limit = 0;
+	}
 	if (ret) {
 		dcp_m4_transaction_fail(&dcp->transaction, ret);
 		dcp_m4_failed(dcp, ret);
@@ -2291,7 +2323,8 @@ static int dcp_m4_boot_framebuffer_check(void)
 static int dcp_m4_startup(struct iomfb_m4 *dcp)
 {
 	struct device *dev = dcp->dev;
-	struct dcp_m4_runtime *r;
+	struct dcp_m4_runtime *r = NULL;
+	ktime_t start = 0;
 	unsigned long flags;
 	u8 output[4];
 	int ret, slot;
@@ -2347,6 +2380,7 @@ static int dcp_m4_startup(struct iomfb_m4 *dcp)
 	/* An external DCP's callbacks may use streams 0..3. */
 	dcp->transaction.external = dcp->external;
 	/* Fresh session only: A401, then the first client open A457. */
+	start = ktime_get();
 	ret = dcp_m4_runtime_call(r, 401, false, NULL, 0, output, sizeof(output));
 	if (!ret && get_unaligned_le32(output) != 1)
 		ret = -EPROTO;
@@ -2381,9 +2415,13 @@ static int dcp_m4_startup(struct iomfb_m4 *dcp)
 	smp_store_release(&r->done, true);
 	dev_info(dev, "IOMFB startup complete: %u callbacks, %ux%u at %u kHz; no modeset\n",
 		 r->callbacks, r->timing.hdisplay, r->timing.vdisplay, r->timing.clock_khz);
+	dev_info(dev, "IOMFB startup took %lld ms\n", ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start));
 	ret = 0;
 	goto out;
 failed:
+	if (start)
+		dev_info(dev, "IOMFB startup stopped after %lld ms, %u callbacks\n",
+			 ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start), r->callbacks);
 	dcp_m4_failed(dcp, ret);
 	dev_err(dev, "IOMFB startup failed: %d; retained until guarded reboot\n", ret);
 out:
